{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if any two numbers in a list are closer to each other than a specified threshold.\n\n    This function implements an optimized approach using sorting, achieving O(N log N) time complexity\n    and O(N) auxiliary space complexity.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold: A floating-point value representing the maximum absolute difference\n                   for two numbers to be considered \"close\".\n\n    Returns:\n        True if at least one pair of distinct numbers (n_i, n_j where i \u2260 j)\n        within 'numbers' exists such that abs(n_i - n_j) < threshold.\n        False otherwise, including when the list has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR-5: Edge Case - If the list contains fewer than two elements, no pair can be formed.\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # PR-1: Time Complexity O(N log N) achieved by sorting.\n    # PR-2: Space Complexity O(N) for storing the sorted copy.\n    # Algorithm Option 2 (Recommended): Sort the list to efficiently check for close elements.\n    # This creates a new sorted list, ensuring the original input 'numbers' list is not modified.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # FR-2: Proximity Check Logic\n    # Iterate through the sorted list, comparing each element with its immediate successor.\n    # If two numbers are \"close\" enough, they must be adjacent in a sorted list.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Calculate the absolute difference between the current element and its next element.\n        diff = abs(sorted_numbers[i] - sorted_numbers[i+1])\n\n        # FR-3: Positive Result\n        # If the difference is less than the threshold, we've found a close pair.\n        # Immediately return True as soon as such a pair is identified.\n        if diff < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    # FR-4: Negative Result\n    # If the loop completes without finding any adjacent pair that satisfies the condition,\n    # then no pair in the entire list satisfies the condition.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Check if in given list of numbers, are any two numbers closer to each other than\n    given threshold.\n    >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 0.5)\n    False\n    >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.8, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0, 2.0], 0.3)\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        if abs(sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i]) < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```"}
